1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,242
Ever wondered how your fridge keeps your food cold, day and night?

2
00:00:04,412 --> 00:00:10,116
Here's the secret: a fridge doesn't make cold at all. It moves heat out.

3
00:00:10,286 --> 00:00:15,318
The trick? When a liquid evaporates, it soaks up the heat around it.

4
00:00:15,488 --> 00:00:21,019
So a special fluid, the refrigerant, travels endlessly in a closed loop of pipes.

5
00:00:21,189 --> 00:00:26,046
Inside, it evaporates at low pressure and pulls heat out of your food.

6
00:00:26,216 --> 00:00:31,618
The compressor sucks in that vapor and squeezes it. Squeezed gas gets hot!

7
00:00:31,788 --> 00:00:36,390
At the back, the hot gas gives its heat to the kitchen and turns liquid again.

8
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:40,894
That's why the back of your fridge feels warm: it's your food's heat!

9
00:00:41,064 --> 00:00:45,573
Then a tiny tube drops the pressure. The liquid turns ice cold again.

10
00:00:45,743 --> 00:00:51,100
And the loop restarts, again and again: heat in on the inside, heat out at the back.

11
00:00:51,270 --> 00:00:55,674
When it's cold enough, the thermostat stops the compressor. Silence!

12
00:00:55,844 --> 00:01:00,771
Well done! Now you know how a fridge really makes cold. Pretty clever, right?

13
00:01:00,941 --> 00:01:05,612
Repair tech tip: leave a gap behind your fridge so its heat can escape.

14
00:01:05,782 --> 00:01:11,649
Cogwise: how things work. Follow for the next one!
